 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method achieves real-time rendering speeds. On the Technicolor dataset, it reaches 64.7 FPS (CDF) and 64.6 FPS (CDF+CCA) on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090 GPU [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Storage is significantly reduced. The CDF component alone reduces model size to a few tens of megabytes [S1]. The full CDF+CCA system achieves approximately 30 MB storage on N3DV and 32 MB on Technicolor [S1].
-*   **Performance:**
-    *   **N3DV Dataset:** PSNR in the low 32 dB range, SSIM approximately 0.94 [S1].
-    *   **Technicolor Dataset:** CDF configuration achieves PSNR 33.29 dB, SSIM 0.912, 64.7 FPS [S1]. CDF+CCA achieves PSNR 33.30 dB, SSIM 0.912, 64.6 FPS, ~32 MB storage [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Superior trade-off among storage efficiency, reconstruction quality, and rendering speed compared to baselines like Swift4D and 4DGS [S1]. Feature caching and dynamic separation maintain real-time performance despite the use of neural decoders [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** The number of grid levels is constrained by GPU memory [S1]. The method requires careful tuning of the hash bucket size `m` to avoid PSNR degradation [S1].
+*   **Storage:** Significantly reduced compared to hash-table-based methods. The CDF component alone reduces model size to a few tens of megabytes. Combined with CCA, total storage is ~30 MB (N3DV) or ~32 MB (Technicolor) [S1]. This is smaller than the lightweight baseline 4DGS [S1].
+*   **Rendering Speed:** Maintains real-time performance. On Technicolor dataset, achieves 64.7 FPS (CDF) and 64.6 FPS (CDF+CCA) on a single NVIDIA RTX 4090 [S1]. Slightly slower than Swift4D's 64.7 FPS due to decoder computation, but comparable [S1].
+*   **Reconstruction Quality:**
+    *   N3DV: PSNR in the low 32 dB range, SSIM ~0.94 [S1].
+    *   Technicolor: PSNR 33.29 dB (CDF), 33.30 dB (CDF+CCA); SSIM 0.912 [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The use of lightweight neural decoders adds computational overhead compared to direct table lookups, but the dynamic-score gating reduces the number of Gaussians requiring deformation computation [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The bucket size $m$ and prime $P$ must be carefully chosen; smaller $m$ noticeably degrades PSNR [S1]. The number of grids is limited to 4-5 due to GPU memory constraints [S1].
 
 ## Background
-CC-4DGS was introduced in the paper "CC-4DGS: Computational Deformation and Point-Cloud Compression for Storage-Efficient Dynamic Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. It builds on prior work such as 4DGS and Swift4D, aiming to address the high storage costs associated with dynamic Gaussian splatting methods that rely on large hash tables [S1]. The method is evaluated on the Neural 3D Video (N3DV) dataset and the Technicolor Light Field dataset [S1].
+CC-4DGS was introduced in the paper "CC-4DGS: Computational Deformation and Point-Cloud Compression for Storage-Efficient Dynamic Gaussian Splatting" [S1]. It addresses the high storage costs of dynamic Gaussian splatting methods that rely on large learnable hash tables (e.g., Swift4D) [S1]. The method builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting representation [S1] and incorporates ideas from Swift4D, such as the dynamic-score strategy [S1]. It was evaluated on the N3DV and Technicolor Light Field datasets [S1].
 
